# Windows Event Viewer Management & Log Analysis

## Overview

As part of my IT Support and Cybersecurity homelab, I practiced using Windows Event Viewer to monitor, filter, and investigate Windows system activity.

The goal of this lab was to understand how Windows records security, application, and system events and how an IT Support technician or SOC analyst can use these logs during troubleshooting and security investigations.

---

## Lab Environment

- Windows Server 2022
- Windows 10 Enterprise client
- Active Directory Domain Environment
- VMware Workstation Player
- Event Viewer

---

## What I Learned

### 1. Windows Event Viewer

Event Viewer is a Windows management tool used to view and investigate events generated by the operating system, applications, and security components.

The major Windows Logs include:

- Application
- Security
- Setup
- System
- Forwarded Events

---

## 2. Security Logs

The Security log contains events related to authentication, account management, privileges, and other security-related activities.

Important Event IDs studied:

| Event ID | Description |
|---|---|
| 4624 | Successful logon |
| 4625 | Failed logon |
| 4634 | Logoff |
| 4648 | Logon using explicit credentials |
| 4672 | Special privileges assigned to new logon |
| 4720 | User account created |
| 4726 | User account deleted |
| 4728 | Member added to security-enabled group |
| 4740 | User account locked out |
| 4768 | Kerberos authentication ticket requested |
| 4769 | Kerberos service ticket requested |

---

## 3. Filtering Events

Instead of manually reviewing thousands of events, I practiced filtering the Security log by Event ID.

Example:

4625

This allows an administrator or SOC analyst to quickly identify failed authentication attempts.

Multiple Event IDs can also be filtered:

4624, 4625, 4740

This allows successful logons, failed logons, and account lockouts to be investigated together.

---

## 4. Filtering by Time

I also practiced narrowing events to specific time periods.

Examples include:

- Last hour
- Last 12 hours
- Last 24 hours
- Last 7 days

This is particularly useful when investigating an incident that occurred during a known timeframe.

---

## 5. Custom Views

I created a custom view called:

Authentication Monitoring

The view focused on:

- 4624 – Successful Logon
- 4625 – Failed Logon
- 4740 – Account Lockout

Custom Views allow frequently used investigations to be saved instead of rebuilding the same filters repeatedly.

I also learned how an organization could create separate views for:

- Authentication Monitoring
- Account Management
- System Health
- PowerShell Activity

---

## 6. XML Event Filtering

Windows Event Viewer provides an XML-based filtering option for more advanced investigations.

Example:

```xml
<QueryList>
  <Query Id="0" Path="Security">
    <Select Path="Security">
      *[System[(EventID=4624)]]
    </Select>
  </Query>
</QueryList>
